"""Concept lessons in syllabus order. The ¶ marker separates teaching paragraphs."""
from teaching.foundations import put

put(14, '''An order service asks a payment provider to charge a card. The provider records the charge and sends a reply. Before the order service receives it, the connection breaks. The customer has made one purchase, but the two services now know different things about it. ¶ A distributed system has participants that communicate and can fail independently. One machine can remain healthy while another stops, and both machines can remain healthy while their messages are lost. Each participant sees its own state and the messages that reach it. It cannot directly inspect everything happening elsewhere. ¶ This changes how we design an operation. We must decide who owns the order, who owns the payment, what each success response promises, and how the services discover an outcome after losing contact. A protocol supplies the rules for sending messages, changing state, acknowledging work, and recovering from interruption. ¶ Additional machines can provide capacity or keep service running through some failures. They also create more combinations of delayed messages and incomplete work. Begin with the requirement that needs distribution, then follow one operation through both its ordinary path and an interrupted path. The connected boxes in a diagram become a design when we can explain these rules. || One logical operation / Several independently failing participants / Caller has incomplete information / Protocol defines allowed outcomes

Follow the payment more closely. First, the order service sends a charge request. Next, the provider commits the charge. Finally, the provider sends a success response, which is lost. The payment succeeded, but the order service has not learned that result. ¶ This is a partial failure: one part of the overall work has happened while another part has failed or remains uncertain. The order service should distinguish “we have not attempted payment” from “we attempted payment but do not yet know its outcome.” Treating both states as a definite failure loses information needed for recovery. ¶ Give the payment a stable operation reference and retain the local intention to charge. If the provider supports looking up that reference, the order service can ask what happened and update the order accordingly. Comparing the local record with the provider's record is reconciliation. Repeating a submission also requires the provider's documented duplicate-handling rules; a reference is useful only when the relevant participants use it consistently. ¶ Rolling back a local database transaction does not remove a charge already stored by the provider. A refund, where supported, is a further business action called compensation. It has its own possible failures and may have different fees or timing. Recovery begins by finding the actual outcome, rather than assuming that a missing reply erased it. || Provider accepts payment / Order service loses response / Payment state is uncertain locally / Reconciliation establishes actual outcome

A service receives a short message from its leader every second. Such a message is called a heartbeat. Five seconds pass without one. The leader may have crashed, but it may also be paused, overloaded, or separated from the observer by a slow network. ¶ From the missing messages alone, these situations can look the same. In a network with no known upper bound on delivery time, waiting longer still cannot establish with certainty that the leader has stopped. A timeout says that the observer is no longer willing to wait. It supplies a practical suspicion of failure. ¶ The distinction matters when the observer starts a replacement. The earlier leader may resume, and a request sent to it may still arrive. Similarly, retrying a timed-out payment can overlap the original attempt. A correctness rule must remain valid when the timeout's suspicion is wrong. ¶ Use deadlines to bound waiting and release resources. Use the ownership or duplicate-handling protocol to decide which effects may occur. Later in this unit, we will see fencing: a resource checks an authority number and rejects work from an earlier owner. While valid authority cannot be established, an operation that needs exclusive ownership may have to wait or reject the request. || Heartbeat delayed / Observer suspects failure / Old participant may still run / Protocol fences conflicting authority

Suppose a replication protocol tolerates a machine that stops responding. Does that also mean it tolerates a machine that sends fabricated answers? These failures give the other participants very different problems. A fault model states which failures the design assumes can occur. ¶ In a crash-stop model, a failed participant stops and does not return. A crash-recovery model allows it to restart. Recovery introduces another question: which information survives? A protocol may require the participant to reload a durable record of its earlier decisions before responding again. If that record disappears, the protocol's assumptions may no longer hold. ¶ A Byzantine failure allows arbitrary behaviour, including sending different, contradictory histories to different recipients. A protocol designed only for crashes need not remain correct in this model. The network model matters too: messages may be delayed, lost, or cut off between groups of machines. A network partition is this loss of communication between otherwise potentially functioning groups. ¶ State these conditions before choosing how many copies, or replicas, to keep. Three replicas sharing one storage device can all lose their data in one device failure. Separately placed replicas face different common causes. Replica counts become meaningful when paired with the failures, storage guarantees, and placement assumptions they are meant to cover. || Choose crash or Byzantine model / State storage-survival assumptions / Define network uncertainty / Evaluate protocol only within model

Machine A sends a message stamped 10:01. Machine B receives it when its own clock reads 10:00. The receive event must follow the send event, even though sorting these two timestamps would put them in the opposite order. The machines disagree about the time. ¶ A wall clock gives a date and time of day. Its reading may differ from another machine's because the clocks run at slightly different rates, called drift, or because synchronization adjusts a reading. These readings are useful for user-visible dates and diagnosis, but unknown clock error limits the conclusions we can draw from them. ¶ For event ordering, we can instead follow dependencies. An earlier event in one process precedes a later event in that process; sending a message precedes receiving it. We can also chain these relations: if a send precedes a receive, and that receive precedes another send, the first send precedes the second. This relation is called happens-before. Events with no such relation are concurrent in this model, even if their physical occurrence times differ. ¶ Measuring a local request duration is another task. Use a suitable monotonic clock, whose reading does not move backward, rather than a wall clock that may be adjusted. Check its behaviour across suspension and restart. For expiry, state which authority judges the time. For a required shared order, use an ordering protocol or log. These choices answer different questions that one timestamp cannot settle by itself. || Node A clock says 10:01 / Node B clock says 10:00 / Messages can still flow A to B / Clock order does not establish cause

A post can carry both “published at 10:03” and “ordering counter 27.” The first field estimates when publication happened in real time. The second records progress according to an ordering rule. The numbers serve different purposes. ¶ A physical clock attempts to measure real time, usually relative to a common time standard. Synchronizing physical clocks reduces their disagreement, but the remaining uncertainty matters. If two timestamps differ by less than the possible clock error, their numerical order may not establish which event occurred first. Subtracting timestamps recorded on different machines can also give a misleading duration. ¶ A logical clock records ordering information. For example, a process can advance a counter whenever an event occurs and include that counter in outgoing messages. A receiving process then updates its own counter using a defined rule. The resulting values describe relationships among events without measuring elapsed seconds. Counter 27 followed by counter 28 does not mean that one second passed. ¶ Use physical time to display dates, a suitable local clock to measure durations, and logical ordering where the protocol needs dependency information. Some schemes combine physical and logical values, but their interpretation depends on their particular update rules. Giving stored fields precise names and meanings helps prevent later code from treating an ordering counter as a time interval. || Physical clock estimates time / Logical clock tracks ordering / Different questions need different clocks / Synchronization error bounds interpretation

Let process A keep one integer counter. Before each local event, including sending a message, it increases the counter. A sent message carries that value. On receiving a message, a process sets its counter to one more than the larger of its current value and the message's value. These are Lamport clock rules. ¶ If the receiver's value is 5 and the message carries 9, the new value is max(5,9)+1 = 10. Under these rules, an event that happens before another has a smaller Lamport value. The converse does not follow: two independent events can receive values 5 and 9 without either depending on the other. The counter preserves a known dependency but does not reveal every dependency from numerical comparison alone. ¶ A vector clock keeps one counter for each participant in a specified participant set. With participants A and B, the first entry records A's progress and the second records B's. Each participant advances its own entry for an event. On receiving a vector, it combines the entries by taking each component's maximum and advances its own entry for the receive event. ¶ Starting from [0,0], an independent event at A has [1,0], while an independent event at B has [0,1]. Compare corresponding entries. Neither vector is at least as large as the other everywhere, so these events are concurrent. If B then receives A's message, the componentwise merge is [1,1], and incrementing B's entry gives [1,2]. The earlier [1,0] is no greater in any entry and smaller in at least one, so it precedes [1,2]. The code example uses exactly these comparisons. ¶ Vector metadata grows with the participant set, and changing that set needs care. Neither kind of clock chooses how two conflicting edits should be merged. They describe event relationships; a business rule and a storage protocol still have to decide which outcomes are valid. || A event has vector [1,0] / B independent event has [0,1] / Neither vector dominates / Merge policy must handle concurrency

A user publishes post P in one region. A friend reads from another region a moment later and does not see it. Is the system behaving correctly? We cannot answer until we know what the read operation promises. ¶ A consistency model defines which histories of operations and observations are allowed. A history records actions such as invoking a write, receiving its success response, starting a read, and returning a value. By examining that order, we can ask whether a result satisfies the chosen model. ¶ Write a small history before choosing a database setting. If publication has completed before the friend's read starts, must P appear? If the friend has already seen P, may a later read omit it? If someone writes a reply after reading P, may another reader see the reply before P? These are different requirements, and different models answer them differently. ¶ Also state the scope: one key, several records in a transaction, one user's session, or the whole service. A delayed popularity count and an exclusive seat allocation can need different rules. Stronger ordering requirements can require communication before a response, and may limit which requests can complete during lost communication. Naming the permitted histories makes those costs easier to evaluate. || User writes post P / Another region reads / Possible absence or presence / Chosen consistency contract defines legality

Suppose region A accepts a post while region B still has the earlier feed. Replication later carries the update to B. If updates stop, communication resumes, and the required repair continues successfully, the copies eventually reach an agreed result. This convergence is the central promise of eventual consistency. ¶ The promise alone gives no maximum waiting time. It also does not choose what to do if both regions accepted different edits to the same record. A system needs a conflict rule, such as retaining both versions for resolution or applying an appropriate merge. Reaching one common value does not establish that the chosen value preserves everything the application needs. ¶ “Strong consistency” is a less precise phrase. It often means linearizability, which we examine next, but speakers and products may use it differently. Ask which operations, observation rules, and failure conditions the claim actually covers. ¶ We can combine requirements deliberately. A friend's feed may allow propagation delay while the author must see their own completed post immediately. That session requirement needs support beyond eventual convergence alone. Freshness and durability also need separate statements: taking time to propagate an update is different from losing an acknowledged update after a failure. Specify both how reads may lag and which failures committed data must survive. || Write arrives in region A / Region B initially lacks it / Replication and repair proceed / Convergence depends on stated assumptions

Consider a shared value initially equal to 1. Client A writes 2 and receives success. Client B then begins a read. If no other write intervenes, a linearizable implementation must return 2. ¶ Linearizability means that every operation appears to take effect at one instant between its invocation and response, with the resulting order obeying the object's rules. It also preserves real-time precedence: when one operation finishes before another begins, the first must come earlier in that order. The imaginary instant is called its linearization point. ¶ If a read overlaps the write of 2, either 1 or 2 may be valid, depending on where the operations can be placed. All operations must still fit one legal history. A read cannot observe 2 and then, with no intervening write, have a later read return 1. ¶ A timed-out write has an unknown result and may still take effect. Linearizability does not turn lost responses into confirmed failures. Providing the property requires suitable write, read, and recovery rules; comparing timestamps or waiting for a majority of replies does not by itself establish those rules. || Write 2 completes / Later read begins / Read must respect completed write / Overlapping operations permit legal order choices

A bank transfer subtracts 10 from one account and adds 10 to another within a database transaction. Other transactions may run at the same time. Serializability requires their results to match some execution in which whole transactions ran one after another. It lets us reason about the transfer as one transaction rather than every possible interleaving of its individual statements. ¶ This requirement does not, by itself, make the chosen serial order follow real time. Suppose transaction T1 writes x=2 and completes. A separate client later runs T2, which reads x=1. With initial x=1 and no other dependencies, this can fit the serial order T2 then T1. That order explains the values but reverses the observed completion-before-start order. ¶ Strict serializability adds that real-time constraint to serializability. It requires T1 to precede T2 in this example, ruling out the old read. Linearizability applies the corresponding real-time reasoning to operations on an object; strict serializability applies it to transactions that may access several objects. ¶ Check the actual transaction boundary. A local database transaction can group the account updates it controls. It cannot automatically include a payment provider's remote charge. Even with strict serializability inside the database, communication with that provider still needs rules for uncertain replies and recovery. || Transaction history has serial equivalent / Real-time precedence is separate / Strict serializability adds that constraint / External effects remain outside local transaction

Suppose process A writes x=1 and then x=2. Process B reads x=0 and then x=2, with x initially 0. One possible combined order is B's first read, A's first write, A's second write, and B's second read. It preserves the order of each process's own operations. ¶ Sequential consistency requires one such common order that obeys the object's rules and each participant's program order. It does not require that order to preserve real-time precedence across different participants. Thus B's first read can appear before A's writes in the common order even if it began later in real time. Linearizability would add the missing real-time constraint. ¶ Causal consistency focuses on dependencies. Suppose a user reads post P and then writes reply R to it. R depends on P. In an application that retains both and tracks this dependency, a view exposing R must also satisfy the visibility requirement for P. The replica may need to fetch P or delay showing R until its dependency is available. ¶ Two unrelated posts have no such dependency. Causal consistency can allow readers to observe them in different orders, whereas sequential consistency requires a common order. Causal tracking alone also leaves conflict resolution to the specified system. Choose the relationships the application needs to preserve, and check that replication actually carries the information needed to preserve them. || User sees post P / User writes reply R to P / Another replica shows R / It must satisfy required causal dependency on P

You change your profile name and receive success. Your next profile read should reflect that write, or an update that legitimately supersedes it. This is read-your-writes: a guarantee about what your subsequent reads observe within the supported session. ¶ Now suppose you have seen profile version 10. A later read takes you to a replica containing only version 9. Returning version 9 makes your view move backward. Monotonic reads rule out this loss of previously observed progress. Monotonic refers to the progress of versions, not the numerical value of a field: a later, valid edit may change an age from 30 to 29. ¶ One implementation can attach a required replication position to the session. When a read reaches another replica, that replica checks whether it has reached the required position. If it has, it can serve the read. Otherwise, it must catch up, route the request to a sufficiently current owner, or report that it cannot currently provide the promised result. ¶ These are session guarantees, so another user's session may still see older data. They do not alone provide global linearizability. Define how the session's progress survives failover or follows the user to another device. Sending a user repeatedly to one replica can help in ordinary operation, but that routing choice alone says nothing about what happens when the replica is replaced. || Session observes version 10 / Next request routes elsewhere / New owner must reach required progress / Do not return earlier version 9

Two replicas store x=1. A network partition cuts communication between them while both remain running. Suppose A accepts x=2 and acknowledges it. A client then asks B to read x. B cannot learn whether A accepted 2, 3, or no write at all. ¶ CAP formalizes this difficulty for an atomic, or linearizable, read/write object in a model that permits communication failures. Its availability requirement says every request reaching a nonfailed participant must eventually complete with the operation's response. It is stronger than a measured uptime percentage. A partition may prevent messages between groups from arriving indefinitely. ¶ B cannot guarantee the correct latest value from its local information. Waiting indefinitely sacrifices availability; answering may violate atomic consistency. Refusing the operation also gives up the required availability for that request. Thus the protocol cannot guarantee both properties in all executions allowed by this model. ¶ Use this result to specify partition behaviour. Exclusive allocation may wait or reject where authority cannot be established. A feed read may serve an explicitly weaker view. CAP does not supply a permanent “choose any two” product classification or determine ordinary-operation latency. || Replicas separated by partition / One side accepts a write / Other side cannot know that write / Serving all reads conflicts with atomic visibility

Even when both regions are healthy, a message takes time to travel between them. Suppose a write protocol waits for acknowledgment from a distant replica before reporting success. That communication lies on the response path, so the client must wait for it. ¶ PACELC names two design questions. If there is a Partition, how does the system trade Availability against Consistency? Else, while communication is functioning, how does it trade Latency against Consistency? The letters help us remember to examine both failure behaviour and everyday response time. ¶ For example, replying after a local commit and copying the update later can shorten the write path. A nearby replica may then serve an older value. Requiring a coordinated write or routing the read to a current owner can preserve a stronger observation contract, with additional communication on one of those paths. The exact effect depends on the protocol; merely waiting for any remote acknowledgment is insufficient. ¶ Review each important operation and configuration. The same service may coordinate allocations while allowing delayed feed reads. Measure the distances, waits, and workload with stated assumptions. PACELC helps expose a trade-off to investigate; its letters do not establish a protocol's correctness or predict a latency number. || Partition behavior decision / Normal-operation coordination decision / Geographic acknowledgment adds delay / Operation-specific contracts guide the choice

Keep a fixed set of three replicas: A, B, and C. Suppose a write waits for acknowledgments from A and B, and a later read asks B and C. B belongs to both sets. More generally, any two groups of two chosen from these three replicas must overlap. ¶ A quorum is a group large enough to satisfy a protocol's participation rule. Let N be the number of replicas in the fixed membership, W the number required for a write, and R the number consulted for a read. If R+W>N, the two sets cannot be disjoint: there are too few replicas to fill both sets separately. If W>N/2, any two write quorums also intersect. For N=3, R=2, W=2, both conditions hold. ¶ This gives the reader access to at least one participant in a completed write's set. It still needs to interpret that participant's evidence correctly. Did an acknowledgment mean the data was durably stored? How are two writers' versions ordered? Can a reader distinguish an accepted version from an unfinished attempt? ¶ Consider an unfinished write that reaches only A. A naive read of A and C returns that new value. A later read of B and C returns the old value because neither has the unfinished write. Both reads contacted two replicas, but the value moved backward. A linearizable protocol needs additional steps or rules to prevent such a history; quorum arithmetic alone does not do so. ¶ Increasing W can reduce the size R needed for intersection, but makes writes wait for more participants. A quorum operation also needs enough reachable participants to finish. Membership changes and temporary substitute replicas require their own rules: the fixed-set proof cannot simply be reused when the sets no longer belong to the same N replicas. || Replicas: A, B, C / Write set: A and B / Read set: B and C / Intersection B is necessary evidence, not full protocol

Leader A pauses while holding responsibility for writes. Other replicas stop hearing from it and elect B. Later A resumes, still carrying its earlier view of ownership, and sends a write. If the resource accepts both A and B as authorities, the system has split brain. ¶ Electing B did not physically stop A. Nor does a lock's expiry force a paused process to stop executing when it resumes. Correct ownership therefore needs enforcement at the place where protected effects are accepted. ¶ One approach uses a fencing epoch: an increasing number identifying a period of authority. Suppose A holds epoch 7 and B receives epoch 8 from the ownership protocol. A storage receiver records the highest accepted epoch and atomically checks that number with each protected write. Once it has accepted 8, it rejects a write carrying 7, including a delayed request from A. ¶ The transition must establish the required fence before relying on the new owner's exclusive effects. The receiver must retain and enforce the authority information according to the recovery contract; an unchecked number in a request provides no protection. Test the sequence “pause, replace, resume,” together with delayed messages. It exposes a competing-owner history that a clean crash test never exercises. || Old leader pauses / New leader obtains higher epoch / Old leader resumes / Resource rejects stale epoch effects

Suppose several workers need to agree which one owns a data partition. Each can read a configuration file, but independently deciding “the old owner seems absent, so I am now the owner” can produce two owners. They need a shared decision with rules for concurrent proposals and failures. ¶ Distributed coordination establishes decisions of this kind. Consensus is the agreement problem underlying many coordination services: participants must not choose conflicting decisions, and a chosen decision must satisfy the protocol's validity rules. A replicated decision log can record an owner and its epoch so participants recover the same authoritative history. ¶ Follow the complete path. The coordination protocol records worker B as owner for a new epoch. B obtains that decision and presents its epoch when acting. The protected resource checks the authority before accepting an effect. Agreement about ownership and enforcement of ownership are separate steps; both are needed. ¶ Coordination also has a progress cost. If participants cannot exchange the messages required for a decision, a safe protocol may be unable to issue new ownership. Define what clients can still do: reading a previously approved, sufficiently fresh configuration may be acceptable, while inventing another exclusive owner is not. ¶ Use shared decisions where an invariant requires them. Two independent updates commute if applying them in either order gives the same result. Where the domain and protocol safely permit such updates, every update may not need one common ordering decision. The choice follows from the state rule being protected, rather than from a general preference for more or less coordination. || Need one authoritative decision / Consensus metadata records epoch / Clients act under that epoch / Resource enforces accepted authority

Imagine replacing a local function call with a call to another team's service. The function used to run in the same process. The new call needs a connection, transfers bytes, may wait in another queue, and can lose its reply after the remote work succeeds. Keeping the old assumptions would hide much of the new operation. ¶ The fallacies of distributed computing name common mistaken assumptions: the network is reliable and secure, latency is zero, bandwidth is unlimited, topology stays fixed, there is one administrator, transport costs nothing, and the network is homogeneous. Here topology means which participants and routes are connected; homogeneous means that the communicating environment behaves uniformly. ¶ Turn the list into questions about this call. How long will the caller wait? What happens after an uncertain result? How is the peer authenticated and the action authorized? Can old and new message versions communicate during a rollout? If the caller requests fifty responses instead of one, how much data and connection capacity does it consume? ¶ Also identify who operates each side and who can change it. A remote team's deployment, certificate rotation, or address change may affect the caller without a local code change. The list provides useful review prompts. The answers still need concrete contracts, resource limits, and recovery rules in the actual design. || Locate remote boundary / Account for latency and loss / Verify security and version assumptions / Assign ownership across administrative boundaries

A booking service promises that one seat will never be assigned to two buyers. If a history ever contains two incompatible successful assignments, the promise is broken. This is a safety property: it excludes a bad outcome at every point in execution. ¶ The service also promises that every accepted booking eventually reaches a recorded outcome, such as confirmed or rejected. This is a liveness property: it requires eventual progress. A booking that remains pending forever can satisfy the no-double-sale rule while failing this progress requirement. A timeout on the customer's screen does not resolve the accepted booking's stored state. ¶ Progress needs assumptions about the environment. We might require that enough participants eventually recover, communication becomes sufficiently timely, and pending work continues to be scheduled. While a partition prevents establishing authority, the service may preserve safety by waiting. It should then resume or recover the operation when the required conditions return. “Eventually” alone supplies no response-time bound; a deadline objective is an additional requirement. ¶ Rejecting every request can also preserve safety while making the service useless. It even satisfies “every accepted booking resolves” when nothing is accepted. To rule this out, state useful-service requirements as well: for example, valid requests for available seats should complete when the required participants are healthy and reachable. ¶ Monitor old pending work and provide a recovery procedure. Operators need a way to discover the existing decision and advance the operation without creating a second owner. A useful design explains both which outcomes must never occur and under which conditions work must move forward. || Safety: no double allocation / Liveness: accepted booking resolves / Partition may require waiting / Recovery must preserve both obligations''')

put(15, '''Suppose our task database runs on machine A, with copies on B and C. A accepts a new task and sends the change to the other two machines. These copies can help us recover if A stops, and they may also serve reads. Keeping copies of a dataset on several participants is called replication. In a primary/replica architecture, the primary is the participant currently authorised to accept writes for that dataset. ¶ Now let A disappear just after telling the user “Saved.” If B has not received the task, promoting B may make a saved task disappear from the user's view. If A later returns and still accepts writes, there may be two competing histories. The arrangement of three machines has not, by itself, settled either problem. ¶ We need a contract for acknowledgment: what must happen before a write receives success? We also need rules for promotion, meaning the transfer of write authority to another replica. These rules must preserve the history the contract promises, restrict which replicas are eligible to become primary, and prevent the old primary from making unauthorised changes. A read from a lagging replica may return an earlier state even while the primary is healthy. Replica count is useful information, but persistence, ownership, and recovery rules determine what those copies let us promise. || Primary owns writes / Replicas receive changes / Acknowledgment policy defines durability / Promotion must preserve authorized history

Let the primary record “create task 7” followed by “complete task 7.” A follower must reproduce this order; applying completion before creation would not have the same meaning. In leader/follower replication, one leader sends an ordered stream of records to followers, which reproduce the changes. ¶ Follow one record through a follower. First, the follower receives its bytes into memory. Next, it writes them to the persistent storage required by the protocol. Later, it applies the record to its task collection. Receipt, durable storage, and application are different milestones. A crash after receipt can lose a memory-only record. A follower that has stored a record but has not applied it can still serve an older task list. ¶ Replication lag describes how far a follower trails the relevant leader position. The relevant position depends on the question: stored records matter for recovery, while applied records matter for what a local read can show. Some protocols also distinguish records that are present from records that have been committed, or accepted into the authoritative history. We will examine that distinction with Raft. ¶ When comparing implementations, ask which milestone a follower acknowledges, what reads it may serve, and which history it may continue after promotion. Saying that a follower “has the data” leaves these questions unanswered. || Leader appends record / Follower receives it / Required persistence is acknowledged / Apply progress controls readable state

Imagine two regions, each with a writable copy of a task. Their connection fails. In region A, a user changes its title to “Read chapter.” In region B, another user changes the same title to “Review notes.” Neither region has seen the other change. When communication returns, both versions must be handled. ¶ Multi-leader replication allows more than one leader to accept writes for the same replicated data. It can make local writes possible during a regional disconnection, but the system must define the result of concurrent changes: changes made without either side having observed the other. One option keeps both versions for review. Another merges changes where the application's data model permits it. ¶ A last-writer-wins rule selects one version using an ordering such as a timestamp with a defined tie-breaker. This can make replicas converge, meaning they eventually agree, while still discarding a meaningful edit. A clock ahead of the others may make an earlier real-world edit appear later. The rule therefore needs a business justification as well as an unambiguous ordering. ¶ Also specify how deletions propagate and how a record already replicated through one leader is recognised, so it does not circulate as a new update indefinitely. Multiple writers are suitable when the conflict rules fit the application. Exclusive allocation, such as selling the final item, may still require coordination. || Region A accepts edit / Region B accepts concurrent edit / Both versions propagate / Explicit merge or conflict resolution decides result

Suppose a coordinator sends a task update to several replicas and waits for the responses required by its write rule. A later read asks several replicas for their versions. One may have an earlier value; others may report the update. The read protocol must interpret these versions and decide which result to return. ¶ Leaderless replication uses an arrangement of this kind instead of routing every write through one permanent leader. A coordinator handles a particular request, but that role need not give it permanent authority over the dataset. The absence of one permanent leader does not remove the need for rules about concurrent updates, membership, durable acknowledgments, or version comparison. ¶ Replicas also need ways to repair missing data. Read repair updates stale replicas discovered during a read. Hinted handoff temporarily keeps information intended for an unavailable replica and forwards it when possible. Background anti-entropy compares replicas and repairs differences outside individual requests. Each mechanism has its own scope; a stored hint is not automatically equivalent to acknowledgment by the original replica. ¶ Trace two concurrent writes and the return of a long-absent replica when evaluating such a system. Two responses out of three provide a count. Whether they establish a fresh, linearizable result depends on the full protocol, including how it handles versions and overlapping requests. || Coordinator contacts replicas / Responses carry versions / Reconcile under documented rules / Background repair restores appropriate copies

A primary stores a task and immediately reports success. Its replica will receive the update later. This is asynchronous replication: the caller's success does not wait for the specified replica work. If the primary is lost before another eligible replica durably stores the task, failover can lose an acknowledged write. ¶ Now change the order. The primary sends the record, waits for the required replica acknowledgment, and then reports success. This is synchronous replication. We still need to ask what that acknowledgment means. “Received in memory,” “stored durably,” and “applied to readable state” are distinct promises. Waiting for receipt alone does not provide the same protection against a replica process restart as waiting for durable storage. ¶ The required replicas matter too. Two durable copies on independent machines protect against a different set of failures from copies that share the same failed storage. A promotion rule must continue a history compatible with the acknowledgment contract; promoting any reachable replica can defeat the intended protection. ¶ Waiting for remote work adds latency. If a required replica cannot communicate, writes may have to wait or fail rather than receive success. Choose this boundary from the application's data-loss and availability requirements, then test the same boundary through failover. The words “synchronous” and “asynchronous” describe the waiting arrangement; the stated milestones determine its meaning. || Write reaches leader / Required replica acknowledgment defined / Caller receives success / Failure loss depends on that boundary

In a fixed group of three voters A, B, and C, a majority contains two members. The group A+B overlaps B+C at B. Any two majorities in this group share at least one member. This intersection gives a later decision a possible witness to an earlier one. ¶ A quorum is a set of participants whose responses satisfy a protocol's decision rule. The shared participant is useful only if it remembers the required evidence and obeys rules that carry that evidence into later decisions. For example, a voter may need to persist a vote before replying, so restarting does not let it forget and vote incompatibly. A participant holding an accepted log record may have to reject a candidate whose history is too old. ¶ If B simply agrees with every request, intersection protects nothing: B can help two overlapping groups accept conflicting histories. The arithmetic establishes a shared member, while the protocol explains what that member must preserve and refuse. ¶ This reasoning assumes the same fixed voter group. Changing the group can change which quorums intersect, so membership transitions require their own rules. Use quorum counts to follow a specified protocol, together with its persistence, eligibility, and recovery rules. A majority count on its own is not an algorithm for consistent storage. || Majority A+B / Majority B+C / Node B intersects / Persistent voting and log rules preserve evidence

Start with an empty task collection on A, B, and C. Suppose they agree that the first command is “create task 7” and the next is “complete task 7.” Each applies creation to the empty collection, then completion to the resulting task. Their task collections now agree. ¶ This is the replicated state-machine approach. A state machine applies a command to its current state to produce a new state and a result. Replication works when replicas begin from equivalent state and apply the same commands in the same order deterministically: the same state and command must produce equivalent results. Consensus lets participants agree on a choice; repeated decisions can establish the command sequence despite the failures allowed by the protocol. ¶ Agreement must preserve safety, so incompatible commands cannot both become the accepted command at one position. Progress also needs conditions, such as enough reachable participants and sufficiently favourable communication. Consensus does not promise continued decisions through every possible partition or endless delay. ¶ Suppose creation calls a random-number generator independently on each replica. The agreed command could then create different identifiers. Put the chosen identifier into the agreed command, or use another supported way to make the decision consistent. The same concern applies to reading the current time. ¶ Applying a command also differs from causing an external effect. If every replica applies “charge the customer” by calling a payment provider, the log alone does not prevent repeated charges. External work needs explicit ownership, stable operation identity, and recovery at the receiving boundary. || Consensus orders command C / Replicas apply C deterministically / States evolve equivalently / External effects need separate coordination

Return to three replicas A, B, and C. They share a committed history through index 8, where an index is a position in the log. A is leader, but a partition separates it from B and C. B stops receiving the expected leader messages. A timeout can make B a candidate: a participant seeking leadership. The timeout creates suspicion, rather than proving that A has stopped. ¶ Raft divides activity into numbered terms, or election generations. In the worked trace, B seeks leadership in term 5. A voter grants at most one vote in a term and checks the candidate's log. Raft compares the term of the last entry first; a higher last-entry term is more up to date. If those terms match, the longer log wins the comparison. Thus a candidate cannot obtain votes merely by being reachable. It must meet the log-eligibility rule as well. The current term, vote, and required log state are persisted before the replies specified by Raft, so a restart cannot erase the evidence on which safety depends. ¶ If eligible B obtains a majority, it becomes leader. It appends index 9 with term 5, sends it to C, and waits for the required durable replication. A follower checks that the preceding log position and term match before accepting the continuation. If its uncommitted suffix conflicts with the leader's history, Raft repairs that suffix under its log rules. ¶ B can use majority replication of index 9 to advance commitment because that entry belongs to B's current term. Commitment establishes the authoritative prefix through that position; the replicas can apply its commands in order. An entry inherited from an earlier term cannot be declared committed merely by counting its copies on a majority. Raft commits earlier entries indirectly when the required current-term entry commits. This restriction works with election eligibility to preserve history through later leadership changes. ¶ A remains unable to commit a new quorum-backed write alone. On learning a higher term, a participant returns to the follower role; the role diagram shows these transitions, rather than the entire algorithm. Reads need protection too. A leader can be isolated and unaware that a replacement exists. A supported linearizable-read path establishes current authority and the required committed position, then waits for local application before serving the result. Election, replication, commitment, reads, and membership must follow the implementation's complete Raft protocol. || Election term advances / Majority selects eligible leader / Leader replicates ordered entries / Current-term commit rule protects history

Consider one decision, such as which command belongs at a particular log position. A proposer asks acceptors to choose a value. An acceptor is a participant that records promises and accepted proposals. Paxos gives each proposal a unique, ordered number, so an acceptor can distinguish an older attempt from a newer one. ¶ First comes preparation. A proposer chooses a higher proposal number and asks a majority for promises not to accept lower-numbered proposals. Each reply also reports the highest-numbered proposal the acceptor has already accepted, if there is one. If any such evidence is returned, the proposer must use the value from the highest-numbered accepted proposal among those replies. If none is returned, it may choose a new proposed value. ¶ Next comes acceptance. The proposer asks acceptors to accept that proposal number and value. An acceptor can accept if doing so does not break a promise it has made to a higher-numbered attempt. Acceptance by the required majority chooses the value. Promises and accepted evidence must survive the failures covered by the protocol. ¶ Why preserve an earlier value? A new preparation majority intersects any earlier choosing majority. Its replies carry evidence that prevents a later successful attempt from choosing a conflicting value. A new proposer is therefore not free to start the decision again with its favourite value. Competing proposers can still keep interrupting one another and delay progress. ¶ Multi-Paxos uses related decisions to build an ordered command sequence; a stable leader can reduce repeated preparation work. This is a conceptual introduction. Precise persistence, recovery, membership, and progress rules remain necessary in an implementation. || Higher proposal prepares / Majority reports accepted evidence / Proposer preserves required value / Majority acceptance chooses under protocol

A replica's log can contain a command that the application is not yet entitled to treat as accepted. It can also contain a committed command that has not yet changed its local readable state. To follow recovery, keep four questions separate: where does the record sit in the order, is it durably stored, is it committed, and has it been applied? ¶ The figure shows a leader in term 5 with records 8:t4 and 9:t5. This notation means index 8 was created in term 4 and index 9 in term 5. One follower has durably stored both; another has only index 8. Under the fixed-membership Raft rules, the leader and the first follower supply the majority needed for the current-term entry 9. Its commitment also protects the preceding log prefix. The second follower can catch up later. ¶ Until commitment is established, the presence of index 9 on a particular machine does not by itself justify a success claim. After commitment, a replica still has to apply the entry before its local task state reflects it. A read requiring that position must wait for the appropriate application progress. ¶ Recovery preserves committed history while repairing incompatible uncommitted suffixes. A snapshot, which stores state representing an earlier applied prefix, must identify a history compatible with the remaining log. Monitor commit and apply positions as well as stored positions: each explains a different kind of delay. || Log entry appended / Entry replicated but not yet committed / Protocol establishes committed prefix / State machine applies committed history

Suppose we replace a cluster's voters by editing a configuration file. The old group still believes that it may decide, while the new group begins deciding independently. If their decision majorities can be disjoint, neither group needs to consult a participant carrying the other's evidence. Both may accept incompatible histories. ¶ Membership is therefore part of the agreement protocol. It specifies which participants have voting authority. A safe change must preserve the required decision rules during the transition, including when messages are delayed or the leader fails halfway through it. ¶ Raft's joint-consensus approach introduces an intermediate configuration containing the old and new groups. Decisions in that stage need the required majority from each group. Only through the supported sequence does the new configuration become authoritative by itself. New servers can first receive the log as nonvoting participants, so catching them up does not immediately change who can decide. ¶ Use the reconfiguration procedure provided by the chosen implementation; some implementations expose a different supported procedure. Preserve persistent member identities and test interrupted transitions. Reusing an identity or replacing all members outside the protocol can invalidate assumptions about remembered votes and logs. Adding a server changes more than storage capacity if it also changes the decision group. || Old voter configuration / Controlled overlapping transition / New members catch up / New configuration becomes authoritative

Worker A is allowed to update a resource using token 7. It pauses for long enough that its lease expires. Worker B obtains token 8 and begins work. When A resumes, it still holds its old instructions and can send a write. Updating a coordinator's owner field has not stopped A's process or withdrawn messages it already sent. ¶ A fencing token is an ordered ownership generation carried with a protected request. The receiver checks the generation before accepting the effect. Suppose the resource records 8 as its highest accepted generation. An incoming request with 7 must be rejected. The comparison and the protected update must be atomic: another request must not slip between the authority check and the change. ¶ The point at which the receiver learns the new generation matters. Merely issuing token 8 at the coordinator does not make a receiver that has only seen 7 reject 7. The design must ensure that the newer generation is established where the effect is accepted, according to its ownership contract. The receiver must also retain the authority information across the failures covered by that contract. ¶ Fencing prevents later actions under obsolete authority once the relevant newer generation is established. It does not undo an effect already accepted, and it does not protect a separate external API that ignores the token. Trace every effect boundary when preventing split brain: the condition in which competing participants act as if each has current authority. || Worker A holds epoch 7 / Worker B obtains epoch 8 / Store records highest accepted epoch / Later epoch 7 effect is rejected

A follower expects heartbeats from its leader. None arrives before its waiting limit. The leader may have crashed, but it may also be paused or separated by a congested network. From the follower's local observation, these situations can look the same. ¶ A failure detector turns observations such as missing messages into a suspicion that a participant has failed. Choosing a shorter timeout can start recovery sooner, but it can also produce more false suspicions. Choosing a longer one may avoid some unnecessary elections while making users wait longer after a real failure. These are choices about detection and recovery speed under assumptions about timing. ¶ The ownership protocol must remain safe when the suspicion is wrong. In Raft, a timeout starts an election subject to voting and log rules; it does not grant leadership immediately. For a worker operating on external storage, resource-side fencing may be needed to reject the old worker after authority changes. ¶ A heartbeat arriving successfully proves little about spare capacity or the latency of an unrelated request. Readiness and load checks answer additional questions. Keep these observations distinct: detection supplies evidence for taking action, while the authority protocol determines which decisions and effects may be accepted. || Heartbeat expected / Delay crosses suspicion threshold / Election or failover may start / Safety survives an incorrect suspicion

A storage system needs to record which worker currently owns a partition. If two workers read incompatible owner records and both act on them, the payload can be damaged even when the payload store itself is healthy. Small records about ownership, configuration, or generations can therefore need stronger coordination than much larger application data. ¶ A strongly consistent metadata service maintains such control information under a specified consistency contract. For a linearizable ownership operation, a successful update must be observed by later operations that require current state, respecting their real-time order. Consensus can support this contract, but a cached read from any replica does not automatically provide it. Use the service's supported write and fresh-read paths. ¶ This service belongs to the control plane: the part that decides how work is assigned and managed. The data plane performs the ordinary reads, writes, or transfers using those decisions. Existing data-plane traffic may continue with safely usable cached state while the metadata service is unavailable. Acquiring a new lease or changing ownership may have to wait because it requires a fresh decision. ¶ State which cached decisions remain valid, for how long, and how stale authority is rejected. Consulting the metadata service on every payload request creates a capacity and availability dependency, so do so where the contract requires it. Its own storage, quorum, and recovery assumptions remain part of the system design. || Small metadata decision / Consensus preserves authoritative epoch / Data plane uses cached safe state / New decisions pause when authority unavailable

Suppose only one worker should update a shared file at a time. A lock grants permission to an identified owner. If the owner crashes while holding it, a lock with no recovery rule could block others indefinitely. A lease addresses this problem by granting permission only for a bounded period, with expiry and renewal defined by the lease service's timing rules. ¶ Now let the worker pause beyond that period. Another worker acquires the lease, but the first worker later resumes at the instruction following acquisition. Its old local variable may still say “I own the lease.” Acquisition and the protected action are separate events; time can pass and authority can change between them. ¶ Use fencing at the protected resource when this history threatens correctness. The receiver must reject the obsolete ownership generation under an atomic authority check. Releasing the lock also needs an ownership check: an old holder must not delete the newer holder's lock merely because the lock has the same name. Renewal needs the corresponding identity and validity rules. ¶ Leases rely on stated assumptions about expiry and time; a local clock comparison alone is not a complete distributed ownership protocol. When the entire invariant lies inside one database, a transaction or database-native coordination may protect it more directly. Choose the coordination boundary around the resource and the failures that can interrupt its use. || Acquire lease with owner identity / Perform bounded protected work / Lease expires or owner pauses / Resource rejects stale owner through fencing

Two disconnected replicas begin with an empty set. Replica A adds X, while replica B adds Y. When they exchange state, each takes the union: the result contains X and Y. Merging again changes nothing. Reversing the exchange order produces the same result. A grow-only set, which permits additions but no removals, gives us a simple example of a conflict-free replicated data type, or CRDT. ¶ CRDTs define update and merge rules so that replicas can converge under stated delivery and state assumptions. For a state-based grow-only set, union is commutative, so merge order does not matter; associative, so grouping does not matter; and idempotent, so repeated receipt of the same state adds no new effect. With the required updates eventually exchanged, these properties make duplicate and differently ordered exchanges manageable. Other CRDT families use different update and delivery conditions. ¶ Removal needs more information. If one replica removes X while another concurrently adds X, simply choosing union cannot express the intended removal. An observed-remove set gives additions distinct identities and tracks which additions a removal actually observed. A concurrent addition that the removal did not observe can survive. Safe cleanup must preserve the information needed to distinguish these histories. ¶ Convergence says that replicas eventually agree according to the type's rules. It does not guarantee every application invariant. Two regions can each allocate the last item they see; merging both decrements can agree on negative stock. Exclusive allocation needs suitable coordination or a design that assigns separate spending rights. Some edits are better retained as conflicts for people to resolve. Choose the merge's meaning before choosing automatic convergence. || Replica A adds item X / Replica B adds item Y / Merge by union yields X,Y / Domain invariants still require separate reasoning

A user writes in one region while another region holds a recovery copy. If the local primary replies before that remote copy is durable, the request avoids waiting for that remote acknowledgment. It also leaves a period in which loss of the local history can lose an acknowledged write. Waiting for a required cross-region durable quorum changes that exposure, while adding remote communication and making some partitions block progress. ¶ Begin a regional design by stating where each dataset may be written and which locations must acknowledge success. A single-authority design needs safe promotion and stale-owner protection. A multi-leader design needs valid conflict semantics. The copies' geography matters: several replicas inside one region do not provide the same regional-loss protection as required durable copies elsewhere. ¶ During a partition, the chosen contract determines which side, if any, can accept authoritative writes. Some stale reads may remain acceptable even when new writes must fail. State this per operation rather than describing the whole application as simply available or unavailable. ¶ Recovery has a return journey too. A repaired old primary may carry a divergent uncommitted suffix or lack records committed elsewhere. Before it can write, it must rejoin under current authority and catch up, reconcile, or rebuild according to the protocol. Keep stale actions fenced and verify routing and capacity before returning traffic. Test failback, the return to a recovered region, as carefully as failover to the replacement. || Regions exchange history / Partition changes reachable authority / Chosen policy governs writes / Recovery and failback reconcile histories''')

put(16, '''A customer places an order. The order service can save the order and reply while receipt delivery continues later. If the request waits for the receipt result, that part of the communication is synchronous. If submission and observing completion happen separately, it is asynchronous. ¶ There are now two completion points. The first response may mean “your order and the obligation to send a receipt are saved.” It does not yet mean “the receipt was delivered.” The response should provide an order identifier and a way to check progress after the request ends. ¶ For the promise to survive a process crash, the receipt intent must become durable before the service reports that it has accepted this work. Keeping a background task only in request memory leaves nobody responsible after a restart. A queue lets workers handle a temporary burst later, but it does not create processing capacity: if arrivals continue faster than workers finish, the backlog keeps growing. || Order transaction commits / Receipt intent becomes durable / Request can return / Worker later records delivery outcome

Suppose several receipt workers are available. A work queue can assign a receipt job to one worker, then assign another job to a different worker. This arrangement distributes work among consumers. A retained log instead keeps an append history that readers can revisit, each using a stored position to find its next record. ¶ Now suppose both a receipt service and an analytics service should learn about each order. Publication and subscription, or pub/sub, describes distributing publications to subscribers. Whether a subscriber can recover messages missed during an outage depends on the system's retention and acknowledgment policy. An ephemeral publication may retain nothing for an absent subscriber. ¶ A product can combine queue, log, and pub/sub behaviour. Choose by the work that recovery requires. A consumer returning after a long outage needs records retained for long enough and a way to recover its progress position. Calling a service a broker does not, by itself, establish that it provides this durable history. || Work queue assigns a job / Log retains ordered records / Pub/sub distributes to subscribers / Recovery depends on configured retention

Follow one receipt event through the system. The order service submits the event; it is the producer. The broker stores or routes it. A receipt worker receives the event and performs the work; it is the consumer. ¶ Several replies can occur along this path. The broker might acknowledge receiving bytes in memory, or it might acknowledge persistence under specified storage and replication assumptions. Later, the consumer can acknowledge receipt or completion of a business effect. These statements establish different facts. A producer that has received a broker reply cannot assume that a customer already has a receipt. ¶ Name what each acknowledgment confirms. Give the message a stable identity, a schema version explaining its format, an owner, and correlation information linking it to the order. Then follow both its age and its business outcome. A broker can be healthy and accepting messages while every consumer rejects their payloads; broker uptime and enqueue rate alone would hide that failure. || Producer submits message / Broker acknowledges defined persistence / Consumer receives record / Business effect commits before its acknowledgment

A retained log may divide records into partitions. Think of each partition as an ordered lane. A key, such as an account identifier, selects a lane so related records can share its order. An offset identifies a position within that partition; a committed progress position tells a consumer where to resume. ¶ A consumer group distributes responsibility for partitions among its members. Suppose a consumer processes offset 42, then stops before saving its progress. After a membership change, another consumer may receive that partition and process the record again. This transfer of partition ownership is a rebalance. It does not erase effects the earlier consumer already performed. ¶ Each partition has its own order and progress. Reaching a particular offset in P0 says nothing about completion in P1. Choose keys from the ordering the application needs, and decide when progress may safely commit. One heavily used partition can limit the whole group while other consumers are idle. Adding consumers does not automatically divide that lane, and parallel work within a lane needs its own effect-ordering rules. || Key selects partition / Group assigns partition owner / Consumer processes offset 42 / Rebalance may replay uncommitted progress

The broker delivers event E7 twice, but the application increments its local counter only once. These observations can both be correct. A delivery is an attempt to pass the record to the consumer. The counter change is the business effect produced while processing it. ¶ To obtain this result, the consumer recognises E7 and commits that recognition together with the counter change. On redelivery, it finds the saved identity and skips another increment. An external email sent during processing would be a further effect, with its own rules for repetition. ¶ A delivery guarantee therefore needs a stated boundary. Are we counting broker deliveries, execution starts, or committed effects? Follow acceptance at the source, retention and redelivery, effect commit, and progress acknowledgment. State what survives the failures under discussion at each step. A guarantee about one of these steps does not automatically describe the entire path from the original request to every business outcome. || Message accepted at source / Record retained or redelivered / Consumer effect commits / Progress acknowledgment defines replay behavior

Consider two possible orders for processing a message. In the first, the consumer acknowledges the message and then updates its counter. If it crashes after acknowledgment but before the update, the broker may consider the work finished and never redeliver it. At this boundary, at-most-once handling allows a missing outcome: zero applications are possible. ¶ In the second order, the consumer updates the counter and then acknowledges. A crash between these events leaves the counter changed but the broker's progress unchanged. Redelivery can start processing again. Under the required retention and recovery assumptions, this supports at-least-once handling, so the application must make repetition safe. ¶ For one local database effect, an atomic inbox can record the processed event identifier and the effect in the same transaction. Either both commit or neither does. Saving them separately leaves another crash gap. We will examine this consumer pattern below. Neither acknowledgment order alone makes an unrelated email or payment call atomic with broker progress. || Ack before effect can lose work / Effect before ack can replay / Atomic inbox protects local effect / External effects need their own contract

Suppose a processor reads an input record, produces an output record, and saves where it has reached in the input. A supported stream-processing transaction can commit the output and consumed progress together. If it aborts, neither becomes committed; if it commits, both do. Recovery can then avoid skipping that input or exposing a second committed output for the same processing. ¶ This is a meaningful exactly-once claim within the records, transaction protocol, and fault model it covers. Now let the processing also call an external email server. The email may be sent before a crash even if the stream transaction does not commit. Recovery can execute the input again and send another email. The external call was outside the transaction. ¶ For an external provider, use the protocol that provider supports. A stable idempotency key may let several attempts produce one relevant outcome within its identity and retention rules, although execution still starts more than once. State which records and effects the guarantee covers, and which failures recovery can handle. || Consume input offset / Transaction couples progress and output / Broker-visible result has scoped guarantee / External HTTP effect remains outside boundary

Two account events have sequence numbers 10 and 11. The log stores and delivers 10 first. Worker A starts processing 10 but waits for a provider. Worker B starts 11 and finishes first. Log order, delivery order, start order, and effect-completion order are different observations; the first two did not establish the last. ¶ If the account rule requires event 10 to take effect before 11, the consumer must preserve that dependency when applying changes. It can coordinate processing by account or use an equivalent sequencing boundary. A later record must not be reported as applied while an earlier required change remains unresolved. ¶ A permanently failing record makes this choice visible. Skipping event 10 may let processing continue, but can break the account's required sequence. Sequence numbers and gap detection help reveal the missing step. Decide the business ordering rule first, then choose partition keys, processing concurrency, and a deliberate policy for records that block progress. || Account event sequence 10,11 / Parallel workers finish 11 first / Commit policy detects dependency / Handle gap before claiming ordered effects

A consumer commits its local update, sends an acknowledgment, and loses contact with the broker. If the broker did not record the acknowledgment, it can redeliver the message. The local update still happened. Moving the acknowledgment before the update would create the opposite risk: a crash could leave the broker finished and the update missing. ¶ An acknowledgment tells the broker that a specified responsibility has advanced. For example, “saved to a durable inbox” can allow later processing to recover from that inbox. “Business effect completed” claims that a later step has finished. In either case, the receiver needs durable evidence supporting the promise through the failures it covers. ¶ Choose the effect boundary before deciding when to acknowledge. Local transactions, inbox/outbox records, stable provider keys, and reconciliation each address particular gaps; acknowledgment alone cannot make a separate external system atomic. Set acknowledgment deadlines or extend processing leases to fit the expected work as well. Otherwise, a healthy but slow worker may repeatedly lose its assignment while it is still running. || Effect starts / Effect commits / Consumer acknowledges / Lost acknowledgment triggers safe replay

A receipt attempt fails because its provider is briefly unavailable. Waiting before retrying gives the provider time to recover and stops one failing job from immediately consuming another worker. An invalid address is different: sending the unchanged request a thousand times will not repair it. Classify failures and bound both attempts and waiting time. ¶ When the ordinary retry policy ends, a dead-letter queue can hold the unresolved work for repair or review. The job has moved to another recovery path; its business obligation has not thereby been completed. Important unresolved work still needs an owner and alerting. ¶ Keep the original event identity, source version, attempt history, and a safe error reason. After repair, replay should continue the same work under its duplicate and ordering rules. A replay tool that invents fresh identities could repeat established effects, while one that skips a required record could break a sequence. The repair path should restore valid progress. || Attempt fails transiently / Backoff and retry within policy / Persistent failure quarantined / Repair and replay preserve original identity

One record fails on every attempt while neighbouring records succeed. Its data may be malformed, its schema version unsupported, or its requested business action invalid. Such a record is often called a poison message because ordinary retries keep encountering the same obstacle. ¶ The response depends on the cause. If a corrected decoder can read a legitimate historical version, a controlled replay may recover the work. If the message requests an action the business rules forbid, retrying the unchanged payload will not make it valid. Before discarding records, inspect samples and failure patterns: a software bug can reject an entire valid version. ¶ Quarantine enough identity and error evidence to diagnose and repair the record, while avoiding unnecessary sensitive payloads. Decide whether records depending on it must wait. Bounded retries protect processing resources, and a review path protects the unresolved business work. During repair and replay, preserve the rules that prevent repeated effects and detect gaps in required ordering. || Decode or validate record / Deterministic failure repeats / Quarantine with bounded evidence / Decide sequence and repair policy

The consumer receives E1 and begins a database transaction. It inserts E1 into a table of processed event identifiers, then increments the orders counter. A uniqueness rule allows only one stored entry for E1. When this transaction commits, both the recognition and the counter change become durable. ¶ Suppose the acknowledgment is lost and E1 arrives again. The attempted insert finds the existing identity, so the consumer skips the increment. If the first transaction had rolled back, neither its identity nor its counter change would be committed, and another attempt could do the work. This is an idempotent consumer for the local effect: repeated deliveries leave the same relevant outcome. ¶ The two writes must share the transaction. Recording E1 first in a separate commit could cause recovery to skip a missing increment; incrementing first could cause recovery to repeat it. Keep the identity for the supported delivery and replay window, and retain it through restart. An external provider is outside this local transaction and needs its own stable key or reconciliation protocol. || Begin local transaction / Insert unique processed-event ID / Apply business state change / Commit both or neither

An order can be represented by its latest state, or by a history of the accepted changes that produced it. With event sourcing, that accepted domain-event history is authoritative. For example, applying OrderPlaced and then later accepted changes under the order's rules reconstructs its current state. ¶ Reconstruction applies the events in order. A snapshot can save time by storing an intermediate state together with the event position it represents; replay then continues from the next event. A broker's transport log is not automatically this domain history. It may retain records for a different period and have different ownership and meanings. ¶ The history must remain interpretable as schemas change, so define how corrections, replay, privacy, and retention work. Rebuilding state or a read projection must not resend real charges or emails whenever an old event is read. Auditability also depends on the domain and its access rules: append-only storage alone does not establish a sufficient financial ledger. Choose event sourcing when preserving and interpreting this history serves a real need. || Append domain event / Fold events into current state / Snapshot records position / Replay rebuilds state without repeating effects

An order command checks whether a requested transition is legal before changing the authoritative order. A customer-history page has another task: show convenient rows of order summaries. Each can be simpler with a model suited to its work. Command Query Responsibility Segregation, or CQRS, separates these command and query responsibilities or models. ¶ A projection builds the read-friendly view from accepted changes. After an order command commits, the projection may still show the earlier state until it processes that change. Define what the customer should see during this interval. The application might read the current authoritative owner, show progress, or accept a stated delay. It also needs a way to rebuild the projection after failure. ¶ CQRS can use one database and one process. Event sourcing, separate databases, and separate services are additional choices. Introduce extra models and synchronisation where they simplify actual validation and query workloads enough to justify maintaining agreement between them. || Command updates authoritative model / Change feeds read projection / Query serves convenient view / Freshness contract explains projection lag

An order commits, but the process crashes before it publishes OrderPlaced. The database contains the order, while downstream services have no event to act on. A transactional outbox repairs this local gap: write the order change and an outbox row describing the event in the same database transaction. A commit establishes both the change and the obligation to publish; an abort establishes neither. ¶ A relay reads the pending outbox row and publishes its event. Suppose publication succeeds, then the relay crashes before marking the row sent. On recovery, the row still appears pending, so the relay can publish the same event again. The durable intent prevents forgotten publication, but it does not by itself prevent repetition. ¶ Keep a stable event identity so consumers can handle duplicates. Monitor the age of pending intents and retain enough history for the required recovery. The original transaction covers local state and publication intent; subscribers and their external effects keep their own transaction, identity, and recovery rules. || Transaction updates order / Same commit inserts outbox event / Relay publishes event / Crash before mark can duplicate publication

Checkout reserves inventory, authorises payment, and requests shipment from separate owners. Each step can commit locally before the next begins. A saga records this sequence and the actions required when the overall operation cannot continue. ¶ Suppose inventory is reserved and payment authorised, but shipment creation fails. The workflow now owes compensating actions: release the stock reservation and cancel the authorisation or request the appropriate refund, according to the payment state. The reservation and payment step really occurred. Compensation is another business operation that moves the system toward an acceptable recovery outcome. ¶ Persist forward and compensating progress. If a refund attempt fails or its reply is lost, the saga remains unresolved until retry or reconciliation establishes the result. A refund may happen later, incur different costs, or leave user-visible consequences; compensation is not always a perfect inverse. When any step's reply is missing, check the relevant authority using its stable identity before assuming nothing happened or starting a fresh operation. || Reserve inventory / Authorize payment / Shipment creation fails / Compensate and track unresolved actions

After inventory is reserved, checkout needs to request payment. With choreography, a participant observes the InventoryReserved event and reacts by starting the next action. With orchestration, an explicit coordinator records “inventory reserved” and decides to issue the payment command. Both arrangements can implement the same business sequence. ¶ Follow where an operator would find the current progress and the next obligation. In choreography, that information is spread across participants and their events, which can make an entire stalled workflow harder to see. Orchestration gathers the decisions about retries and compensation into a coordinator, while adding that coordinator and a workflow definition that must evolve safely. ¶ Neither arrangement removes participant failure or the need for durable outcomes. Choose according to the sequence's complexity, audit needs, and teams changing it. Carry a shared correlation identity through either design so an operator can connect each participant's records to the same business operation and work out where it stopped. || Participant events drive next action / Orchestrator alternatively issues commands / Both need durable participant outcomes / Observe one correlated business workflow

A workflow requests human approval and waits. Its executor then restarts. If progress and the outstanding wait are durable, a replacement can continue waiting for the same approval instead of beginning checkout again. A workflow engine provides mechanisms for persisting progress, timers, and retry decisions under its supported programming model. ¶ External activities still have separate failure points. Suppose an activity sends a payment request, the provider accepts it, and the worker crashes before recording completion. The recovered workflow knows that it dispatched the activity, but may not know the provider's result. Running the activity again can repeat the call. A stable activity identity and effect key let recovery relate attempts to the original intention and reconcile with the provider. ¶ Understand the engine's replay, determinism, versioning, activity-identity, and cancellation rules. The application must still define legal transitions and safe external actions. Durable coordination makes long-running recovery easier within that model; it does not automatically establish one external effect for every activity. || Workflow state persisted / Activity dispatched / Result may be uncertain after worker loss / Resume with stable activity identity

A durable job record keeps the intended work's identity, input reference, progress, attempt history, and replaceable owner. The job is the business intention. An attempt is one worker's try at carrying it out. ¶ Suppose attempt seven stops responding, loses authority, and attempt eight begins. Attempt seven may later resume. Before accepting its result, storage must check that the attempt or fencing epoch is still current and that the result belongs to the expected input version. A conditional completion can then reject the obsolete worker's write. This check protects the result where it is enforced; external effect receivers need suitable protection too. ¶ Store large inputs separately and verify their version before processing. Bound retries and expose terminal or review states when automatic recovery cannot finish. Returning HTTP 202 does not itself give a process-local background task durable progress or a replacement owner. The accepted response must agree with the persisted job and with who will continue it after a crash. || Persist job and input version / Worker claims attempt epoch / Work produces versioned result / Conditional completion rejects stale attempt

A report is due at noon. Two schedulers both observe that it is time to run. Give the occurrence a stable identity, such as (schedule_id, occurrence), and use an atomic claim or uniqueness rule to create one durable run for it. Later worker retries are attempts of that run; they are not new noon occurrences. ¶ A worker may hold a lease that grants authority for a limited period. If its lease expires while it is paused, another worker can take over, yet the old worker may resume too. Lease expiry does not stop its code. Protect accepted effects with the appropriate idempotency or fencing checks at the resource that receives them. ¶ Define what happens after missed periods, clock changes, overlapping runs, and scheduler failover. One scheduler process does not by itself make occurrences unique or recoverable across restarts. Deciding that an occurrence is due and safely executing its effects are separate stages, each needing durable identity and recovery rules. || Due occurrence identified / Atomic claim assigns epoch / Worker executes stable run ID / Expiry and recovery preserve effect identity

A consumer returns after an outage and replays retained input. Its old records may call the same database or provider that is currently serving live requests. If replay uses all available capacity, current users can wait even though the recovery worker appears productive. ¶ For an illustrative capacity budget, suppose the dependency can handle 100 operations per second and live work needs 70. At most 30 operations per second remain for replay under those assumptions; allocating all 100 to replay leaves no room for the live work. These figures are teaching inputs, not measured capacity. Backpressure limits admission or slows producers when downstream work cannot keep up, while a separate replay budget limits catch-up work. ¶ Preserve source identities and suppress or separately control repeated external effects during replay. Retention must cover the required recovery interval. Monitor oldest unfinished age and blockage by key as well as queue length and throughput. Many completed records can hide one important operation that has remained stuck. || Consumer capacity limited / Producer slows or queue stays bounded / Replay uses separate budget / Completion age remains observable

The local payment record says pending, but the provider has charged the card. This can happen when the provider accepts the charge and its reply is lost before local completion is saved. A fresh charge would risk paying twice. ¶ Reconciliation compares the intended local operation with the observed external outcome. Use the original stable operation identity to query the provider. If evidence confirms the charge, apply the matching local transition under the usual uniqueness and state rules, and record the evidence. If the evidence is incomplete, keep a pending investigation state. Recovery is advancing the existing intention rather than inventing another payment to clear a pending row. ¶ The reconciler must be authorised, idempotent, and observable. Run reconciliation periodically and during recovery, and track unresolved discrepancies by age and owner. This is a normal part of handling partial failure. It still depends on correct persistence and validation on the main path: without the original identity and intent, there may be no safe basis for comparison. || Local intent remains uncertain / Read provider outcome by identity / Compare with local invariant / Repair once and record evidence''')

put(17, '''Two workers use the same disk and credential source. Their processes are separate, but one disk failure or credential change can stop both. A failure domain groups parts exposed to a common cause. ¶ List those shared causes as well as the separate processes. If both workers run on one host, losing that host removes both workers' CPU and disk access. Even on separate hosts, a credential rotation can deny both access to the same provider. A process boundary protects against some failures while leaving these others shared. ¶ Fault isolation limits how a failure spreads. For example, separating the resources used by payment and receipt delivery can keep a stalled email provider from occupying every payment worker. Map the shared hosts, zones, dependencies, and administrative controls before choosing a boundary. Separation costs capacity and operating work, so choose it according to the harm and recovery objective, then test the shared dependencies as well as individual processes. || Process boundary / Shared host and disk / Zone and dependency correlation / Isolation targets an explicit failure cause

A database refuses requests during recovery but keeps all committed rows. Users cannot obtain service during this period, although their committed data remains recoverable. A web application can show the opposite problem: it keeps answering requests but loses writes that it had accepted into memory before a restart. ¶ Availability concerns whether users can obtain the required service when they need it. Durability concerns whether accepted, committed information remains recoverable through the stated failures. We need separate evidence for these promises. A successful response does not establish that a write will survive a restart; a recoverable database does not establish that it can serve requests now. ¶ Define which requests count and what a useful response means, then state the data-loss tolerance and failures covered. During a careful restore, stopping service may help preserve the correct history. Traffic failover may restore access, while backups may recover data. Neither measure alone establishes the whole reliability contract. || Request success indicator / Committed-state retention test / Recovery duration objective / Separate evidence for separate promises

Two instances each operate at 70% of their individual capacity. If one fails, the remaining instance cannot absorb both workloads at the same rate. Redundancy provides additional instances or copies, but it needs enough surviving capacity to carry the promised workload. ¶ For illustration, assume each instance can handle 100 requests per second while meeting the response-time target. Two running at 70% carry 140 requests per second together. After one fails, the survivor can complete only 100 at that assumed capacity. The remaining forty requests per second need a permitted reduced-service or rejection policy. A queue can absorb a short burst, but it will keep growing if this excess continues. ¶ The replacement also needs usable state, independent failure placement, failure detection, and traffic movement. Shared bad configuration can disable every replica at once. Test takeover and restoration under the expected load. Spare resources help when the system can find them, direct work to them, and preserve the required state during the transition. || Two instances at 70% / One instance fails / Remaining load exceeds capacity / Headroom or shedding preserves essential service

In active-active operation, several locations or instances serve the selected function at the same time. Active-passive keeps a standby ready for takeover. The choice can differ by layer: application instances may all serve requests while one database leader owns writes. ¶ A standby may hold a data copy without accepting authoritative writes. To promote it, we must establish whether its history meets the data-loss policy and prevent the earlier owner from writing again. This prevention is called fencing. In an active-active arrangement, define which actions different participants may accept concurrently and how conflicting changes are prevented or resolved. ¶ A passive standby can have configuration drift or warmup problems that appear only during takeover. Multiple active instances need routing and suitable coordination. For either arrangement, define failure detection, surviving capacity, and the return after repair. Test that the replacement can reconstruct usable state and serve real requests. The operating model includes these transitions as well as the normal traffic arrangement. || Normal serving topology / Standby or concurrent active owner / Failure transfers responsibility / Test data authority and return path

A client permits two seconds for an operation. Spending three seconds waiting for a database connection would exceed that allowance before the query even starts. An overall deadline marks the time after which the request is no longer useful to its caller. ¶ Record the deadline when the request begins. Before each stage, calculate how much time remains. Waiting for a pooled connection, making remote attempts, backing off between attempts, and returning the reply all consume the same allowance. A stage timeout limits a particular wait; it should fit within the remaining overall time. Giving every stage a fresh full timeout can keep work running long after the caller has stopped waiting. ¶ Pass the remaining time downstream and cancel or clean up owned work when possible so it releases resources. Record which stage timed out to distinguish waiting for a connection from executing a query. A timeout does not prove that an external write failed: the provider may have completed it before its reply was lost. That uncertain effect needs recovery even though the caller's wait has ended. || Establish end-to-end deadline / Allocate remaining stage budgets / Cancel owned work on expiry / Reconcile uncertain remote effects

A dropped connection during a safe lookup may justify another attempt. An invalid payment amount will not improve if the same request is repeated. Before retrying, decide whether the failure is likely to be temporary and whether repeating this operation is safe. ¶ Backoff increases the wait between attempts. With exponential backoff, the wait grows by a factor after each failure, usually up to a chosen maximum. Jitter varies these waits so callers are less likely to retry together. These techniques change timing; a maximum attempt count, an overall retry budget, and a deadline bound the extra work. ¶ If an effect may already have happened, retain a stable operation identity and use a repetition or lookup rule supported by the receiver. An identifier alone cannot make an arbitrary payment call safe to repeat. Respect relevant retry guidance and decide which layer owns retries. Several independent policies can multiply attempts around one request. Include retry traffic in capacity planning, because it competes with new work and recovery. || Classify failure / Check safe identity and remaining time / Wait randomized bounded backoff / Retry or surface explicit outcome

Four nested layers each allow three attempts, including the initial attempt. One logical request can then create up to 3⁴ = 81 downstream attempts. During a dependency outage, this amplification can consume the capacity that might otherwise complete useful work. ¶ Draw a call tree. The outer layer can make three attempts. Each can cause three attempts at the next layer, giving nine. Repeating this through four layers gives 3×3×3×3 = 81 attempts at the bottom. This is a worst-case allowance: success, a deadline, or another limit may stop the tree sooner. ¶ Coordinate retry ownership and enforce a budget across the whole path. Jitter spreads attempts in time but does not reduce their configured total by itself. Bound admission and suppress redundant work where possible. Measure original user intents separately from attempts and useful completions; a rise in traffic during an outage may come from retries rather than new users. Leave capacity for successful work and recovery. || One intent enters four layers / Each permits three attempts / Potential downstream attempts: 81 / Limit retries across the whole path

A dependency repeatedly fails. A circuit breaker can temporarily reject new calls, preventing each caller from holding resources while waiting for another likely failure. Its common states are closed, open, and half-open. ¶ While closed, the breaker permits calls and observes their outcomes. When its configured failure rule is met, it opens and rejects selected calls promptly. After a wait, it becomes half-open and permits a limited number of recovery probes. Successful probes can close it again; failed probes can reopen it. Define the outcomes and thresholds that control these transitions. ¶ Bound probes across callers and avoid having all instances probe simultaneously after a shared timer. Otherwise, recovery can trigger another surge. The breaker controls resource expenditure; it does not repair the dependency or choose an acceptable business result. A failed authorisation call must not become a fabricated payment approval. The application still needs a deliberate failure, pending, or degraded response whose meaning fits the operation. || Closed allows calls / Failure threshold opens breaker / Open rejects quickly / Half-open probes bounded recovery

Receipt email stalls and occupies every worker. Payment authorisation now cannot obtain a worker even though its provider is healthy. Bulkheads reserve or separate resources so one kind of work cannot consume the entire allowance needed by another. ¶ Give receipt delivery its own bounded workers and connections, with a separate allocation for payment work. If email stalls, it can occupy only its allocation. Bound its waiting queue too, so a growing receipt backlog does not exhaust memory. Payment work can then continue within its reserved capacity, provided the resources it still shares remain available. ¶ Check those shared limits: two worker pools may still exhaust one database, network link, or host. Their combined limits must fit the shared capacity. Too much partitioning can leave resources idle in one pool while another rejects work, so choose boundaries according to workload and harm. Observe saturation and rejection in each pool. Isolation must cover the resource through which the failure would spread. || Critical payment pool / Separate optional email pool / Email stalls and saturates / Payment retains its reserved capacity

Suppose a caller may send occasional bursts, but we need to limit its continuing request rate. A token bucket records an allowance of tokens. Tokens accumulate at a configured refill rate up to the bucket's capacity, and each admitted request spends tokens. ¶ Start a bucket with ten tokens and a refill rate of two per second. Ten immediate unit-cost requests can use the initial burst. If no more requests are admitted, three seconds later the balance is six tokens; it can never exceed the bucket's capacity. Coordinate the balance update and spending decision so simultaneous callers cannot both spend the last token. Excess requests may be rejected. A shaper may instead delay work to control its pace, using bounded waiting. ¶ Define the caller identity, request cost, burst size, and total service budget. Per-tenant limits can support fairness, but their combined allowances must also protect shared capacity. If the limiter's remote state becomes unavailable, decide whether to reject, allow, or spend a previously allocated local budget. These choices have different availability and abuse consequences, so test that failure path. || Identify caller and operation / Consume allowed token atomically / Reject or delay excess / Enforce global and tenant budgets

Under overload, a service may stop generating recommendations while preserving order status and reconciliation. Load shedding rejects selected work early enough to leave resources for higher-priority outcomes. ¶ Rejecting before expensive parsing or remote calls can protect more capacity than rejecting after that work has already been done. Choose priorities from product and correctness requirements. Reconciliation may need reserved capacity to discover the outcomes of already accepted payments, even while optional recommendations are being refused. Return a clear overload result and bound subsequent retries so rejection does not simply create more work. ¶ Shedding can preserve useful completions while demand exceeds capacity; it cannot serve all of an indefinitely excessive workload. More capacity, less work per request, or a changed service target may still be needed. Measure useful accepted outcomes and fairness across callers. Raw throughput can fall while essential work improves, because the service has deliberately stopped spending resources on lower-priority work. || Detect bounded-capacity limit / Admit essential work / Reject optional excess early / Observe goodput and fairness

A product page can remain useful without recommendations. A sale cannot safely proceed merely because an old cached count says stock exists. Graceful degradation preserves an agreed subset of useful behaviour while keeping the rules that still matter. ¶ Define that reduced result before the incident. If recommendations fail, the page can still show the product description and price. If the system cannot establish permission to allocate stock, it may need to pause purchase confirmation. Showing an optimistic confirmation would create a promise that the system cannot support. ¶ Specify which data may be stale, which actions pause, and what the user sees. Then test the reduced path, including its permissions and capacity. Such paths may be used rarely in ordinary operation and fail when first needed. Catching every error and returning a default does not establish safety; the alternative outcome needs a clear meaning of its own. || Recommendation service fails / Catalog remains authoritative / Page omits optional recommendations / Purchase invariants remain enforced

A cached help page may substitute for an unavailable content service. A default account balance cannot establish the state of an unavailable ledger. A fallback is an alternative behaviour whose meaning must be acceptable for this operation. ¶ The fallback is another path through the system, with its own data age, permissions, and load limit. If every failed cache request goes to one small database, the substitute can become the next outage. Check what all callers will do together, as well as whether one fallback request succeeds. Specify how long the alternative remains acceptable and make degraded operation visible where needed. ¶ Switching payment providers also requires care. If the first provider may already have charged the customer, sending the payment to another provider can create a second charge. The alternative must account for the first attempt's identity and uncertain outcome; changing providers does not settle that history. Evaluate a fallback as a business outcome and a recovery path, rather than treating any returned response as success. || Primary dependency fails / Check whether substitute is valid / Serve bounded alternative or reject / Record degraded result and freshness

A process keeps restarting because its liveness check calls an unavailable shared provider. Restarting cannot repair that provider. If every replica follows the same rule, they can all restart together and add warmup work during the outage. ¶ Keep each probe focused on the decision it controls. Liveness asks whether the process needs restarting, for example because it is stuck. Readiness asks whether traffic should be sent here now. Removing readiness can stop new traffic, while separately arranged draining lets ongoing work finish. A startup check or allowance gives initialisation time before ordinary checks apply. ¶ Bound probe work and avoid making every process restart depend on an external service it cannot repair. Readiness policy also needs care: marking every replica unready for one shared dependency may remove service that could still operate in a permitted reduced mode. A successful probe is evidence about that check, not proof that every critical operation meets its objective. Monitor real useful operations alongside these local signals. || Startup initializes required local state / Readiness admits suitable traffic / Liveness detects stuck process / Business SLIs observe actual outcomes

An incident begins at noon. Service returns at 12:20, so the interruption lasted twenty minutes. If recovery restores data only through 11:55, there is five minutes of data-loss exposure relative to the incident point. ¶ The recovery time objective, or RTO, is the target time to restore the defined service. The recovery point objective, or RPO, specifies the acceptable gap between the incident and the recoverable data history, often expressed as time. In our example, twenty minutes and five minutes are observed results to compare with the targets. They do not tell us whether the business considers that recovery acceptable. The five-minute gap describes exposure; actual lost records depend on what was written in that interval. ¶ Set both objectives for the business function. Then measure detection, decision, restoration, validation, and traffic return. Timing only a file copy misses much of the interruption. A faster restore may use older data, so evaluate recovery time and data-loss exposure separately. || Last recoverable point: 11:55 / Incident: 12:00 / Service restored: 12:20 / RPO exposure 5 min; recovery 20 min

A backup survives the lost host, but its only decryption key was on that host. The bytes exist, yet the application cannot recover them. A useful backup needs the surrounding restoration resources too. ¶ A restore needs usable bytes, required keys and credentials, compatible software, configuration, and an environment in which the application can run. Document schema versions and any retained logs needed to reach the required recovery point. Keep these resources independent enough from the failed domain to remain usable. A rehearsal can find missing pieces while ordinary service still runs. ¶ Restore actual backups and check business invariants and reconciliation state, rather than relying only on a completed backup job or valid checksums. Live replication may repeat a mistaken deletion or corruption, while a retained historical backup may recover an earlier state. State which failure each copy protects against and test the corresponding procedure. A copy is useful recovery evidence only when the application can use it to restore the required service and history. || Backup captured / Independent storage and keys retained / Restore into clean environment / Validate application and reconciliation state

Failover moves service or write authority to a replacement. Failback returns it after repair. Both transitions need valid history, sufficient capacity, routing, and protection from conflicting owners. ¶ Before moving writes, establish which destination holds the required history under the data-loss policy and which owner may act next. Fence the old owner so that a delayed or restarted process cannot continue writing. After repair, the old primary must catch up or be rebuilt from the new history before regaining authority. Sending traffic back too soon can expose older data or allow two competing writers. ¶ A database promotion alone does not move every user request. Existing connections, cached addresses, and retries may continue using old paths. Test the full sequence: transfer authority, route traffic, validate useful service, and later return after repair. Include the old owner's attempted return in the test. Recovery is incomplete if that return can reintroduce incompatible writes. || Detect and choose promotion / Fence old authority / Route and validate service / Rebuild old owner before safe failback

Copies in separate zones can survive some local incidents. Copies in separate regions can cover other failures, but add propagation delay, transfer cost, data-placement concerns, and recovery work. ¶ Ask which failure each placement should survive. Separate zones can reduce shared local infrastructure risks. A remote region can protect against a regional incident while adding distance between data copies. Waiting for a distant copy can increase write latency; not waiting can leave recent writes unavailable there when failover is needed. The chosen replication and promotion rules must meet the stated recovery objective. ¶ Check dependencies that remain shared. A global credential change or configuration fault can still affect all regions. Plan capacity for surviving traffic and catch-up work, rather than only the normal traffic split. Geographic distance provides part of the independence. The authority protocol, usable data, and operational dependencies determine whether the remaining location can actually provide the promised service. || Host failure / Zone failure / Regional dependency failure / Each requires a deliberately independent boundary

Begin a fault exercise with a question: if a worker pauses past its lease, can its old completion overwrite a new result? A lease gives authority for a limited period; a pause can leave the worker acting on authority that has already expired. ¶ State the expected invariant, introduce a bounded fault, and check the result. Pause the first worker, let a replacement gain authority, then resume the first and attempt completion. The protected store should reject the stale result. Observing that both processes eventually run again would not establish that the invariant held. ¶ Chaos engineering tests such hypotheses by introducing controlled faults. Define the affected scope, stop conditions, observations, recovery owner, and environment appropriate to the risk. Random process killing without a hypothesis may cause harm without clarifying a mechanism. Increase scope when the evidence justifies it. A passing drill supports the tested scenario and configuration; it does not prove every future failure harmless. || State reliability hypothesis / Inject bounded fault / Observe invariant and recovery / Stop and repair when limits are exceeded

Suppose optional calls consume most of a request's deadline and cause most failures. Removing those calls from the critical path may improve the user experience more directly than adding another region. ¶ Use the service-level objective, or SLO, to identify the required useful outcome. Break down which eligible requests miss it and why. If an optional dependency dominates those failures, removing its wait may address the measured problem. Compare that change with the costs and new failure paths of another region. The error budget—the amount of failure allowed by the objective over its measurement period—helps show how quickly current failures are consuming the allowance. ¶ Keep important low-volume operations visible. Their failures can disappear inside a denominator dominated by cheap successful reads, so separate indicators where the harm differs. More redundancy has cost and complexity; choose it for a failure mechanism it can improve. An error budget helps prioritise work, but a small event count does not make a severe payment or data-loss incident harmless. || Measure meaningful SLI / Compare budget consumption / Locate highest-impact failure mode / Choose justified reliability investment

A slow provider holds connections. Waiting requests accumulate, callers retry, and the extra work overloads previously healthy components. This feedback creates a cascading failure: an initial problem spreads through the resources and dependencies that connect the system. ¶ The first slowdown reduces useful completions. Waiting work retains resources for longer, so fewer resources remain for new requests. Callers then retry, adding demand to the same reduced capacity. A healthy component can become overloaded even though it had no original fault. Interrupting this loop requires reducing admitted or repeated work and releasing scarce resources, while preserving an explicit outcome for obligations already accepted. ¶ Use deadlines, bounded admission, retry budgets, isolation, and permitted degradation at the resource boundaries involved. Leave capacity for reconciliation and recovery. A breaker that sends every failed request to one fallback can start another cascade. Trace the original and recovery paths under a shared failure; testing dependencies one at a time can miss the interaction that turns a modest slowdown into a widespread outage. || Provider slows / Connections and queues accumulate / Retries multiply offered work / Admission and isolation break the loop

After an incident, reconstruct what happened, which conditions allowed it, how it was detected, and what helped or delayed recovery. Stopping at “human error” leaves unanswered why one action could affect every replica. ¶ A timeline separates the initiating event from the conditions that let harm spread. A mistaken command may be the trigger; broad permissions and shared placement may explain its reach. Connect corrective work to those observed mechanisms. Scoped credentials can reduce exposure, a tested restore can improve recovery, and bounded retries can limit amplification. The incident evidence should explain why each action is useful. ¶ Give each action an owner and a way to verify completion. Replaying the relevant failure sequence can show whether the changed system now limits the harm. An added alert helps only when it leads to a useful response. The aim is to reduce recurrence or impact by changing the system and its operating conditions, with evidence that the change addresses what happened. || Reconstruct evidence timeline / Identify contributing system conditions / Assign concrete corrective work / Verify the changed failure behavior''')
